          <Pad Id="HqJy6bKXlzOgrEuW4Xmw1L" Bounds="-620,-60,540,1100" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="02 - A dot, a path, a plot of ground.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Three shapes are on the renderer, and every number that made them is on this canvas.&#xD;&#xA;Change any number - the shape moves the same frame.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THREE KINDS, AND THEY ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS:&#xD;&#xA;  Point        where something is     - and that is all it knows&#xD;&#xA;  LineString   the shape of a way     - it can answer: how long?&#xD;&#xA;  Polygon      a piece of ground      - it can answer: how much?&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Each column is the same story told twice. The Vector2s you already know feed Skia on the&#xD;&#xA;right; the very same Vector2s become Coordinates, and then geometry, on the left. Nothing is&#xD;&#xA;converted back - the drawing and the geometry are two readings of one set of numbers.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE RING CLOSED ITSELF. You gave the polygon four corners. Read its WKT: five pairs, the&#xD;&#xA;last equal to the first. A ring is a list that comes back to where it started, and NTS&#xD;&#xA;closes it so you cannot forget to. Skia's Polygon node asks for the same thing with a&#xD;&#xA;Closed pin - same idea, two APIs, and the toggle beside it proves the point.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;PLAY&#xD;&#xA;  Drag corner numbers until the plot overlaps the path. Nothing complains - geometries do&#xD;&#xA;  not know about each other yet. Chapter 03 is where they start asking.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Make a bowtie: swap two of the polygon's corners. The fill and the Area stop agreeing&#xD;&#xA;  about what is inside. IsValid [NTS.Geometry] is the referee - wire it to the ring and&#xD;&#xA;  read its Reason.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Give the path one more vertex: a Vector2 pad, and one more input on BOTH Cons nodes.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHAT HAPPENED?&#xD;&#xA;  Length and Area are in this canvas's own units, and they are honest here because you own&#xD;&#xA;  the space. The day these numbers become longitude and latitude, Area comes back in square&#xD;&#xA;  degrees - a unit nobody wants. That trap is real, and Act II walks into it on purpose.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS&#xD;&#xA;  LineString    an ordered list of coordinates - the order IS the shape&#xD;&#xA;  LinearRing    a LineString that returns home; the closed boundary of an area&#xD;&#xA;  Polygon       a ring promoted to a surface: it has an inside&#xD;&#xA;  vertex        one coordinate of many&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;CREATIVE CONNECTION&#xD;&#xA;  Spread&lt;Vector2&gt; is what you already draw with. A LineString adds one promise: the order&#xD;&#xA;  means something. A Polygon adds another: inside exists. GIS geometry is your geometry,&#xD;&#xA;  with promises.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THIS PATCH USES ONE PACKAGE, VL.NetTopologySuite, which is normally a reason to live in&#xD;&#xA;that package's own help folder rather than here. It is the exemption written into this&#xD;&#xA;pack's rules: the geometry act runs on that package alone, and a course with a hole in its&#xD;&#xA;opening act is worse than a node appearing in two places.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT - three directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 03              in or out - the first question two geometries answer together&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 07              these same three kinds, drawn on the earth at Haneda&#xD;&#xA;  HowTo Create a polygon   the ring lesson again, one package deep">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
        </Canvas>
